Osteomalacia
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Diffuse bone pain, Proximal muscle weakness, Bone tenderness on palpation, Elevated alkaline phosphatase/Looser zones

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-031
Reason for Visit: Severe, diffuse bone pain preventing ambulation, and a recent low-impact fall due to leg weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute exacerbation of severe, generalized bone pain leading to an inability to walk safely and a reported mechanical fall today. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude acute pathological fractures, provide potent analgesia, and determine if acute hospitalization was necessary before standard imaging facilities opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My whole body aches deep in the bones, my legs gave out on me today, and it hurts just to have my chest touched."
History of Present Illness: Over the past six months, the patient reports a progressive, diffuse, poorly localized bone pain, particularly severe in the lower back, pelvis, and bilateral lower extremities. Today, the patient suffered a low-impact fall, citing profound proximal muscle weakness ("my thighs just stopped working"). The patient describes a waddling gait prior to today's fall. They note generalized severe fatigue and lethargy. The patient is largely housebound due to the progressive weakness and has a severely restricted diet low in dairy and fortified foods. They complain of recent intermittent muscle spasms in the calves.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound following a fall today; unable to rise from a seated position without significant upper body assistance.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk. Poor nutritional status.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Frail, chronically ill-appearing, and wincing with minor repositioning in bed.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe bone tenderness upon firm palpation of the sternum and anterior tibiae bilaterally. No obvious gross deformity, shortening, or external rotation of the lower limbs to suggest an acute displaced neck of femur fracture, though range of motion in the hips is globally painful. Neurological exam confirms profound symmetrical proximal muscle weakness (power 3/5 in hip flexors and shoulder abductors) with preserved distal strength. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Diffuse bone pain, Proximal muscle weakness, Bone tenderness on palpation, and anticipated Elevated alkaline phosphatase/Looser zones pending urgent radiography and laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Dimly lit house with heavy curtains drawn, confirming a severe lack of natural sunlight exposure.
Medication Reconciliation: Occasional paracetamol, which is ineffective for the current pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Osteomalacia with acute functional decline - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Occult Femoral Neck Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is diffuse and bilateral rather than localized to a single groin, and the patient has profound systemic bone tenderness and proximal myopathy, though a fracture cannot be definitively excluded without an X-ray.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite bony tenderness on palpation (sternum/tibia) rather than pure muscular stiffness, and the chronicity of the diffuse bone pain.

Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While it presents with bone pain, the classic symmetric proximal myopathy and specific dietary/sunlight deprivation history strongly favour profound vitamin D deficiency osteomalacia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute functional collapse driven by profound proximal myopathy and severe diffuse bone pain, culminating in a mechanical fall. Triage accurately flagged the inability to mobilize and the potential for a concealed insufficiency fracture. Objective assessment confirmed severe bone tenderness and proximal weakness, classic for advanced osteomalacia. Delaying assessment forced the patient to remain in severe pain and risked further traumatic falls if they attempted to mobilize unassisted overnight. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to provide bridging analgesia, assess for obvious acute fractures, and coordinate an urgent diagnostic workup, satisfying the urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral tramadol 50mg for acute bone pain. Advised strict bed rest overnight. Arranged an urgent outpatient comprehensive metabolic panel (including 25-hydroxyvitamin D, calcium, phosphate, ALP, and PTH) and plain radiographs of the pelvis and bilateral femurs for the following morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring you sit outside in direct sunlight for 15 minutes every morning with your arms exposed (action), your skin will naturally synthesize its own vitamin D (cause), which is the critical first step in pulling calcium back into your softening bones to stop the pain (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community physiotherapy pending clearance of acute fractures on X-ray.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bedside commode recommended to eliminate the need for ambulation tonight.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to interpret X-rays and blood tests, and to initiate high-dose cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3) replacement therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden, sharp, localized pain in the hip or groin, an inability to move one leg entirely, or a sudden loss of bowel or bladder control, call 000 immediately for ambulance transfer to the emergency department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe bone pain and weakness are highly suggestive of a severe vitamin D deficiency causing your bones to soften. X-rays are required tomorrow to ensure no hairline fractures occurred during your fall today.
Clinical Course: With high-dose vitamin D and calcium replacement, the muscle weakness typically improves within weeks, but bone remineralization and complete pain relief will take several months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk to the bathroom tonight; use the commode or a bedpan. Do not take any further pain medication that makes you drowsy to avoid further falls.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A severe lack of vitamin D has prevented your body from absorbing calcium from your diet, forcing it to pull calcium out of your bones to survive. This has left your bones soft and painful, and your muscles severely weakened. The blood tests and X-rays tomorrow will guide the exact medical replacement required.
Actionable Steps: Rest strictly in bed tonight. Attend the radiology clinic using wheelchair transport first thing in the morning.

